{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_423","type":"reasoning","text":"The existing G01 S04 L05 evidence files are unregistered — same state as my new G02 file. This is a pre-existing pattern: these files exist in the tree but are not in retainedFiles, so `check-repository-artifacts.mjs` would flag them in a git checkout (since they're in reviewedDirectories `docs` and not retained). Wait — but that would mean CI is failing at base. Let me check: the reported failure requires the spec to declare docs.required: true with docs.files. Do the existing G01/S04/L05 specs declare docs.files?\n\nEarlier I saw harness-gap-error-recovery-next-action.md has docs.required: true with files: [g01-error-recovery-next-action.md]. So yes, the retention gate would flag it. So at base, the retention gate already fails in a git checkout? That seems unlikely for a repo with CI.\n\nWait — actually in my isolated copy, `git add -A` was run but the harness-gap-closure evidence docs are ignored by .gitignore (/docs/** with only specific !negations), so they were NOT tracked. `check-repository-artifacts` requires docs.files entries to be tracked AND exist AND not local. So an untracked, ignored evidence file fails.\n\nBut the `git ls-files` in my copy showed only 10 harness-gap-closure files tracked (the ones with the ! negations). So g01-error-recovery-next-action.md is untracked/ignored → fails.\n\nThis means the base repo similarly has the G01/S04/... evidence files untracked? But they physically exist in the working tree I received. How did they get into the working tree if they're untracked and ignored? They were placed there by the harness/task setup (they are part of the \"clean candidate\" state). The task workspace is a file snapshot, not necessarily a full git checkout.\n\nHmm. This matters: is the retention gate passing or failing in the real repo? Given CI runs `node scripts/check-repository-artifacts.mjs`, and the real repo tracks whatever `git ls-files` shows. If these evidence files are genuinely tracked in the real repo, then `git add -A` in my copy would have skipped them because .gitignore ignores them... but `git add -A` doesn't add ignored files. In the real repo they'd have been force-added (as `retainedFiles`? no, they're not in retainedFiles).\n\nActually, the key insight: `isLocalArtifact` returns true for any path under `reviewedDirectories` (docs) unless in retainedFiles. And `checkRepositoryArtifacts` flags \"Local artifact is still tracked\" for tracked paths that are local artifacts. So if g01-error-recovery-next-action.md were tracked in the real repo, the gate would fail with \"Local artifact is still tracked\". And it's ignored by .gitignore (from `/docs/**`).\n\nSo in the real repo, these files must be untracked+ignored local files that exist only in this working snapshot. That's consistent: the task snapshot includes local (untracked) evidence files that the retention gate treats as local-only. That means at base, running the retention gate in the real repo would also report \"Spec requires local or missing documentation\" for G01 etc. — unless the real repo has them tracked.\n\nHmm, but this can't be: CI would fail. Let me reconsider. Maybe in the real repo, these files ARE tracked because they were force-added at some point, but then the .gitignore block (generated by `--write-ignore`) doesn't include them, so `git ls-files -i -c --exclude-standard` would list them as \"Tracked path is ignored\" → gate fails. That's also a failure.\n\nGiven the repo is stated to be in a healthy-ish state (harness:ci exists), likely these harness-gap-closure evidence docs are NOT actually tracked in the canonical repo, and the local snapshot contains extra untracked files that are part of the delivered candidate but reside in an ignored directory.\n\nWait — but I read the files (g01-error-recovery-next-action.md etc.) as regular files, plus the SHA256SUMS manifests list them. And `docs/dev/harness-gap-closure` was added to .gitignore with `!` negations only for 10 files. This strongly suggests the *intended* tracked set is those 10 files, and everything else in that dir (all the per-packet evidence) is local-only in this snapshot.\n\nBut then the task's targetScope explicitly lists creating `docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/g02-spec-doc-gardening.md`, `g02-acceptance.json`, `G02-SHA256SUMS` and the G01 precedent shows the same pattern, plus `harness-gap-*.md` deliverable evidence files exist for all other packets. So the repo evidently ships these evidence files as untracked local artifacts at delivery time and their specs' docs.files point to them — which would trip the retention gate in a real CI checkout.\n\nHmm, this is getting deep. Let me step back. The acceptance criteria for G02 don't include `check-repository-artifacts`. The task says \"run relevant existing checks that do not install dependencies\". The retention gate requires git; the workspace has no git, so it inherently can't be part of the \"clean candidate\" acceptance here. I already reported it and it's pre-existing (G01, S04, L05, etc. all fail identically at base). My G02 file is in the same category as those — I should surface this as a residual, not claim a pass.\n\nActually, let me verify more precisely that the retention failure is pre-existing by checking whether the base snapshot (before my edits) also fails. My edit only ADDED g02 files and edited lifecycle lines. The base already had g01/s04/... unregistered. So yes — pre-existing.\n\nBut wait, is it truly pre-existing, or is the *whole* evidence tree supposed to be registered? Let me re-read: the `checkRepositoryArtifacts` loop only checks spec docs.files. The O01 spec's docs.files only lists `docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/o01-approval-rate.md` (registered). G01's spec lists `docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/g01-error-recovery-next-action.md` which is NOT registered → would fail.\n\nLet me actually see if maybe these files are tracked in the real repo by checking whether the retention gate at the *base commit* would fail. I can simulate: in my git copy, the base commit `git ls-files` showed only 10 tracked in that dir. That's because `git add -A` skipped ignored files. But in the real repo they could have been force-added. If force-added, `git ls-files -i -c --exclude-standard` would flag them → still fail.\n\nEither way, the retention gate fails for the pre-existing G01 evidence file. Therefore this is a pre-existing condition of the snapshot, not something I introduced. I'll note it as residual.\n\nHmm, but actually — hold on. Let me reconsider whether the harness-gap-closure evidence files are *really* ignored. The .gitignore has `/docs/**` then `!/docs/**/` then later `!/docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/` and `!/docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/<file>` negations. `git check-ignore` earlier reported g01-error-recovery-next-action.md matched `/docs/**` → ignored. So yes ignored.\n\nSo the pre-existing state: many harness-gap-closure evidence .md files are ignored/untracked, and their specs declare them in docs.files → retention gate fails. This is a genuine pre-existing repo-state issue in this snapshot.\n\nGiven the task's acceptance criteria (#6): \"干净候选定向测试、harness validate/dry-run 通过，凭据扫描 0 命中，SHA256SUMS 可回读\" — retention isn't listed. And the instruction says don't fix unrelated pre-existing bugs. I'll report it honestly.\n\nActually — wait. I should double check: maybe the retention gate is expected to be run and the O01 file was the only one registered because the others were added later without registration, i.e., a real regression that G02 could legitimately be expected to catch? No — targetScope doesn't include scripts/repository-artifacts.json or .gitignore. So I must NOT touch those. Good — leave as residual.\n\nLet me now finalize. Let me re-verify everything with one consolidated check and also confirm the new rule doesn't accidentally break any \"every rule names executable check\" style test. There's no such global test. \n\nLet me also double check the `harness list` includes the new rule and that `docs/README.md` index requirement (docs-index.test.ts) — that test only checks top-level docs/*.md files linked from docs/README.md. My new doc is under docs/dev/, not top-level, so fine.\n\nAlso check-local-paths scans AGENTS.md and docs/**/*.md for developer-local absolute paths. My new doc has no such paths. Good. But let me run it if git-free... it needs git ls-files. Skip; already verified via findMachineLocalPaths that my doc is portable.\n\nLet me produce final summary and RESULT.json.\n\nActually — the task says \"return the required structured result\" referencing .rsi/RESULT_SCHEMA.json which requires status/summary/testsRun. I should output that JSON, likely also write it somewhere? The schema file exists in .rsi/. Let me output it in my final message. Possibly also write .rsi/RESULT.json? The instructions: \"return the required structured result\". I'll output JSON in final message.\n\nLet me run the final consolidated checks once more and gather exact outputs/exit codes.\n\nLet me also double-check: does `harness:specs:lifecycle` get exercised in harness:ci? Yes (package.json line 102 + harness:ci includes it). Good.\n\nOne more: the acceptance says \"文档引用可解析\" — validated. And \"AGENTS.md <=400 and points to harness/specs\" — my test asserts it; already passes.\n\nLet me run a final check batch and then compose the answer.\n\nAlso I realize I should double-check the new rule file is picked up by harness:ci's structural checks? harness:ci validates scenario + example spec, and lists. Not rule-specific. Fine.\n\nLet me verify no `.d.mts` typing surface is required for the rule (rules don't have d.mts). Confirmed only 4 rules have d.mts? Let me not worry.\n\nFinal verification run."}}
